<section class="ritual-section ritual-section--large">

  <div class="ritual-container">

    <div class="ritual-editorial-grid">

      <!-- IMAGE -->
      <div class="ritual-editorial-image ritual-image-soft ritual-fade-up">

        {% if section.settings.image != blank %}

          <img
            src="{{ section.settings.image | image_url: width: 1600 }}"
            alt="Human Rituals"
            loading="lazy"
            width="{{ section.settings.image.width }}"
            height="{{ section.settings.image.height }}"
          >

        {% endif %}

      </div>

      <!-- CONTENT -->
      <div class="ritual-editorial-content">

        {% if section.settings.heading != blank %}
          <h2 class="ritual-heading ritual-fade-up" style="margin-bottom:60px;">
            {{ section.settings.heading }}
          </h2>
        {% endif %}

        {% if section.settings.body != blank %}
          <div class="ritual-body ritual-fade-up">
            {{ section.settings.body }}
          </div>
        {% endif %}

      </div>

    </div>

  </div>

</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Human Rituals",
  "settings": [

    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "Editorial Image"
    },

    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Why Humans Create Rituals"
    },

    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "body",
      "label": "Body",
      "default": "<p>Human beings have always used rituals to create continuity between states.</p><p>Long before modern wellness culture existed, rituals helped people orient mornings, close evenings, regulate uncertainty, process transitions, and create rhythm within daily life.</p><p>Many rituals were not originally designed for optimization or productivity. They existed to help the body and mind move coherently between different emotional, social, environmental, and physiological states.</p><p>The body responds deeply to repetition. Familiar environments, repeated sensory cues, and recurring behaviors gradually create psychological and physiological continuity.</p><p>Rituals were never only about symbolism.</p><p>They also shaped pacing, orientation, transition, and continuity within everyday life.</p>"
    }

  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Human Rituals"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}